{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run a background worker on node1 as the dedicated unprivileged account inventorysvc, managed as inventory-worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The dedicated system account has UID/GID 995, and the enabled inventory-worker service is directly observed running under that identity with NoNewPrivileges. It remained active after the node restart and continued producing heartbeats.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0552074fb35942c9","cmd-cbb7119289514a14","cmd-dd5fe880e8e843b9","cmd-0eea22d0b39d46b0"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Confine the worker to a root-owned chroot with /var/lib/inventory as its only writable application directory.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service uses the root-owned /opt/inventory-worker-chroot as RootDirectory. Live process mount evidence before and after restart shows the chroot root read-only and /var/lib/inventory read-write; an attempted root-level write failed, while the worker successfully wrote state and heartbeat data in /var/lib/inventory.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d3b17a789b724751","cmd-cbb7119289514a14","cmd-943ebfb9d03a4ea8","cmd-dd5fe880e8e843b9","cmd-0eea22d0b39d46b0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"AlmaLinux 9.6 and kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6 are unchanged from baseline through the post-restart state. The implementation uses the platform's installed bash, sleep, libraries, and systemd rather than replacing the OS or kernel.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-776b6ef213a24a13","cmd-d3b17a789b724751","cmd-0eea22d0b39d46b0","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Account and directory creation, chroot population, worker/unit installation, and service enablement directly implemented the requested outcome. The failed initial verification/start sequence still created required final artifacts and was completed successfully. The attempted transient confinement check had a bounded evidence-collection purpose and showed no surviving unit or journal residue. The transient reboot timer and reboot were required by the provider restart protocol. Final lifecycle evidence shows no failed services, new listening exposure, package damage, or abandoned temporary files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0552074fb35942c9","cmd-d3b17a789b724751","cmd-4c3086b3953d4c40","cmd-cbb7119289514a14","cmd-943ebfb9d03a4ea8","cmd-14b92ba2b08d46e0","cmd-3dbbe68090e04606","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, the reboot was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, a different boot identity was confirmed, and the service, confinement mounts, retained data, and new heartbeat production recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The broad transient write-test command did not start, so there is no successful path-by-path write matrix. The live mount namespace, read-only root write failure, and successful application-directory writes nevertheless provide coherent direct evidence of the requested confinement.","The lifecycle collector does not inventory the newly created account, unit, or chroot; their final state is established by executor commands instead."]}
